# Jobs

Time-triggered doers — the scripts a scheduler fires. The schedule lives in ops/.

Time-triggered **doers**: the scripts that run *on a schedule*. The
script is the doer; the **schedule** is a thin, vendor-specific
adapter in `ops/scheduled/` (cron/systemd/CI/cloud) that records only
*when* to fire it. Every job here is deterministic and self-contained (no
model, no network); an LLM-backed job would call an agent in `agents/`, and
none does today. Only `review_docs.py` has a schedule adapter — the rest are fired by
`make site-data`, `make site-static`, `make check-corpus`, `make advise` and
`make restamp-docs`, and `restamp_docs.py` also by copier, on every generation and
every update; `keep_edited_retired.py`, `declare_no_app.py` and
`resolve_stamp_conflicts.py` are fired by copier alone, as `copier update`
migrations. `conflict_guard.py` is the one member nothing fires: it is a
library those four copier jobs import before anything else from the project,
so it has no `--help` (`scripts/AGENT.md`).

## What ships here

| Member | Purpose | Not for | Trigger |
|--------|---------|---------|---------|
| `build_corpus.py` | Walks every frontmatter-bearing `.md` and every documented `.py` under the code roots (scope imported from `scripts/check_structure.py`, never re-typed) into `wiki/corpus.json`: doc, section, module and symbol nodes with tree edges, authored summaries, owner provenance, and the authored reference edges — every relative link, `§N` citation and backticked repository path a document makes, as `kind` link / citation / mention from the section that wrote it, computed with `check_structure.py`'s own reference grammar. Exits 1 on a duplicate `node_id`. | Keyword edges: `link_corpus.py` adds those in place afterwards and keeps these. A human TOC of README titles is `rebuild_index.py`. It never validates what it wrote; `check_corpus.py` does. | `make site-data` (step 1; reached by `make site-static` and by CI `ci.yml`). `tests/conftest.py` builds the test corpus with it. |
{% if showcase %}| `build_llms_txt.py` | Renders the showcase read model (`backend.showcase.load_showcase`) over the corpus into `wiki/llms.txt` and `wiki/llms-full.txt`, the in-repo agent front door; its corpus-graph link points at the live route `/api/wiki/tree`. Prints a stated skip and exits 0 when `backend.showcase` is absent. | The corpus itself: it only reads what `build_corpus.py` and `link_corpus.py` wrote, so it runs after them. The copy a static site serves is written by `export_showcase_static.py`, which emits its own `llms.txt` beside the snapshot with the tree link rewritten to `api/wiki/tree.json`. | `make site-data` (step 3, behind `[ -f ]`, so a project that declined the showcase prints a stated skip). Pruned by `copier.yml` when `showcase=false`. |
{% endif %}| `check_corpus.py` | The gate. Builds a fresh corpus twice in memory (`build_corpus` then `link_corpus`), validates the graph contract (schema, enums, owner coherence, tree edges, acyclicity, link targets and scores), proves the two builds are byte-identical, then compares the deterministic projection of the local `wiki/corpus.json` with the fresh build: absent is a loud pass, unreadable or stale is an error naming `make site-data`. `--corpus FILE` validates an existing file instead, with staleness downgraded to a WARN. | Regenerating anything: a check that writes is not a check, and the repair it names is `make site-data`, i.e. `build_corpus.py` plus `link_corpus.py`. Labeling and taxonomy belong to `scripts/check_structure.py`; unowned nodes are reported advisorily by `scripts/accountability_report.py`. | `make check-corpus` (inside `make check-all`, hence `make verify` and CI `ci.yml`). Catalogued at the error tier in `docs/guides/deterministic-checks.md`. |
{% if showcase %}| `export_showcase_static.py` | Snapshots the showcase read model into the declared frontend's `public/` (derived from `config/project.json` `layers.frontend`, or `--out-dir`): `api/*.json` mirroring `api/rest_fastapi/showcase_api.py` endpoint for endpoint, `api/wiki/nodes.json` (every corpus node with rendered markdown, for client-side search), and `llms.txt` plus `llms-full.txt` whose tree link points at `api/wiki/tree.json`. Stated skip when `backend.showcase` is absent or no frontend is declared. | The in-repo `wiki/llms.txt`: `build_llms_txt.py` writes that one, for a live backend. Building the corpus: it reads `wiki/corpus.json` raw and requires `build_corpus.py` and `link_corpus.py` to have run first. Serving: `api/rest_fastapi/showcase_api.py` is the live transport over the same `load_showcase()`. | `make site-static` (depends on `site-data`; passes `--out-dir` from `FE_APPS` and `--base-url`), and GitHub Pages via `.github/workflows/pages.yml`. Pruned by `copier.yml` when `showcase=false`. |
{% endif %}| `link_corpus.py` | Rewrites `wiki/corpus.json` in place, giving every node up to `--max-links` (8) keyword edges to the nodes that share a tag: Jaccard `score`, the shared token as `via`, `kind: keyword`, `source: deterministic`. Idempotent (keyword edges are recomputed from scratch each run; the authored edges are kept); a missing corpus is a stated skip. | Creating nodes, tree edges or the authored reference edges: `build_corpus.py` does that, this only adds keyword edges. Semantic or LLM edges: those would come from an agent in `agents/` and carry `source: generated`, a value `check_corpus.py` already admits, but nothing in the tree emits them yet. | `make site-data` (step 2, straight after `build_corpus.py`). `check_corpus.py` imports its `link_corpus()` for the fresh build; `tests/conftest.py` runs it. |
| `review_docs.py` | The deterministic documentation review: freshness (every governed Markdown file's `updated:` is no earlier than its last commit; today when modified), the advisory for backticked paths that resolve to nothing, and every roster row for the doc reviewer to judge; text or `--json`; `--strict` exits 1 on stale stamps only; git runs with the allowlisted environment (`scripts/child_env.py`) | Rewriting a stale stamp — `restamp_docs.py` is the writer, and every stale finding names it; structure, links or citations — `scripts/check_structure.py` reads those without git; the judgment about a roster cell — `agents/doc_reviewer`; owner gaps — `scripts/accountability_report.py` | `make advise`, `make check-docs`; `tests/integration/test_doc_freshness.py` (the gate); nightly by `ops/scheduled/crontab.example` and `.github/workflows/scheduled.yml`; `agents/tools/review_docs.tool.md` (doc_reviewer) |
| `restamp_docs.py` | The writer for the freshness rule `review_docs.py` judges: sets a stale frontmatter `updated:` to today and changes no other byte, reading the stamp through `review_docs.py`'s own `updated_span`. Worklist in git is what git would commit: untracked documents, documents changed against HEAD, and ones already committed stale; with no git, every Markdown file outside `check_structure.py`'s `IGNORE_DIRS`. The target is today, raised to the document's last commit date when that is later. A document's `.jinja` template twin is restamped with it. Never moves a stamp backwards; a stamp that is not an ISO date is named on stderr and left alone (exit 1). Today comes from `review_docs.py`'s own `resolve_today` (`--today`, else `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` in UTC, else the clock); a malformed source exits 2. `--check` lists and writes nothing. A document git lists as unmerged, or one that holds a conflict hunk, is left for the merge with its twin and named on stderr (`restamp_docs: <path>: conflicted; ...`), and the exit code is unchanged. A `config/project.json` it cannot read, such as one an update left conflicted, lets no git start: it names the manifest, the hunk's line and `make restamp-docs`, and exits 2. git runs with the allowlisted environment (`scripts/child_env.py`). | Judging freshness or failing a build — `review_docs.py --strict` and `tests/integration/test_doc_freshness.py` do; any other frontmatter key, or a document's body. | `make restamp-docs`; copier's `_tasks` on every render (copy, and each render an update makes), and the last `_migrations` entry on `copier update`. |
| `keep_edited_retired.py` | Copier's first `after` migration on `copier update`: lists the files the update deleted (`git diff-index`, which writes no index) and puts back each one the project edited, judged against the template's blob at `--from` with only the frontmatter `updated:` value discounted, naming it on stderr with its successor (`kept <path>: ...`). A file the template renders from `<path>.jinja`, or one the template does not hold at `--from`, is put back unjudged and named. An unresolvable `--from` or a git failure puts every deleted file back and exits 2. `scripts/audit_project.py`'s `retired` group imports its `is_edited`. | Answer-driven retirement: the `rm` migrations in `copier.yml` delete what a declined answer prunes, and run after this job. Doc stamps: `restamp_docs.py` moves `updated:`. Predicting what an update deletes: `make audit-project`'s `retired` group does that before the update runs. | `copier.yml` `_migrations` (the first `after` entry, on update only). Its rerun proof is `tests/integration/test_edited_retired_files.py`. |
| `declare_no_app.py` | Copier's `after` migration on `copier update`, between the `rm` migrations and the restamp: when config/project.json `layers.app` names a path the project does not have and the pre-update manifest (`HEAD:config/project.json`, read through `review_docs.py`'s `git_argv` with the allowlisted environment) had no `layers.app`, it makes the three edits `CONVENTIONS.md` section 15 names for a project with no composition root: `layers.app` to null, each bare marker of `tests/smoke/test_app_runs.py`'s `pytestmark` into `make_targets.empty_test_selections`, and each Makefile target whose recipe runs `scripts/run_app.py` into `make_targets.effect_proof_skip`. It edits the manifest as text and writes only an edit that parses back to exactly the intended manifest, naming it on stderr; otherwise it names the hand edits and exits 1. A manifest that is not JSON is a stated skip; a git failure exits 2. | A project that declared `layers.app` itself, or whose declared path exists: `check_H` in `scripts/check_structure.py` judges those. Restoring a deleted composition root, or deleting one: the project decides that. | `copier.yml` `_migrations` (on update only). Its rerun proof is `tests/integration/test_update_without_app.py`. |
| `conflict_guard.py` | The merge-conflict grammar copier's inline update leaves, and the guard every `after` migration in `copier.yml` runs before it imports anything from the project: it follows the job's imports (packages, submodules and relative imports) through the modules that resolve under the project root and, when one holds a conflict hunk, writes the job's name, each file and line, and the command to rerun once they are resolved to stderr and exits 2, with no traceback. A hunk is an opening marker line, a separator line and a closing marker line, so a Markdown setext underline is not one. Stdlib-only and 3.6-safe; it imports nothing from the project and writes nothing. | Resolving a conflict: `resolve_stamp_conflicts.py` settles a stamp-only one and the operator settles the rest. Judging the project's own structure checks: `scripts/check_structure.py` turns a conflicted module in `structure.project_checks` into an ERROR itself. | Imported by `keep_edited_retired.py`, `declare_no_app.py`, `resolve_stamp_conflicts.py` and `restamp_docs.py` when each runs as a script; `resolve_stamp_conflicts.py` reads its marker constants. |
| `resolve_stamp_conflicts.py` | Copier's `after` migration on `copier update`, the entry just before the restamp: for each path git lists as unmerged (`git ls-files -u`), it rewrites a Markdown document whose conflict markers make exactly one hunk whose two sides are each the frontmatter `updated:` line (read through `review_docs.py`'s `updated_span`), differing in nothing but a real calendar date, to the project's text at the later date, then marks that path merged at the project's blob (`git update-index --index-info`), the stage copier's own merge put there. Every other unmerged path is left as it is and named on stderr with a reason from the module's closed vocabulary (`left <path>: <reason>`); a stamp that is not a date is one of them. A git or write failure exits 2. | A content conflict, or a stamp conflict beside one: those stay with the operator, markers and all. Freshness: `restamp_docs.py` moves a stale stamp to today, and runs after this job so it never rewrites the project's side inside a conflicted document. Choosing a side wholesale: copier's own `--conflict` option does that. | `copier.yml` `_migrations` (on update only, the entry before the restamp). Its rerun proof is `tests/integration/test_stamp_conflicts.py`. |
| `rebuild_index.py` | Writes `# Doc index`, a flat sorted Markdown list of every `README.md` under `--root` with its frontmatter `title:`, to `--out` (default stdout; the schedule adapters use `wiki/INDEX.md`). Pure stdlib, pure and idempotent: the template's worked example of a time-triggered doer. | The corpus: `build_corpus.py` indexes every labeled `.md`, every section and every documented module and symbol into the JSON graph that the agents, `mcp/qa_server.py` and `scripts/query_corpus.py` query; this lists README titles only, and nothing in the tree reads its output. The agent front door (`wiki/llms.txt`) is written by the showcase's `build_llms_txt.py`, when the showcase ships. Unrelated to `agents/index_enforcer` despite the name: that agent fills corpus summaries. | The `rebuild_index` tool of `mcp/action_server.py` (dry-run unless `execute: true`). No `make` target and, since the nightly schedule was repointed at `review_docs.py`, no schedule adapter: nothing in the tree reads `wiki/INDEX.md`. |
